the end  of an era from today commercial coal  mining in england is effectively over  the bradley mine in county durham has  operated for almost 200 years  but now the open cast pit that produces  150  000 tons of coal a year will close  i'm devastated i'm absolutely devastated  that it's coming to a close this will be  the last coal mine that i'll probably  ever work on  that will be it say what now  i haven't got a clue my partner's family  are in it  they've been in it for 30 years i'm  obviously coming with late  my father like i say work with british  steel that industry's gone um  it's it's awful i think that there's  there's british lads there  that can do the job we've got all the  all the machinery  got all the training probably more  qualified than a lot of the rest of the  world  but we've had it all taken away from us  there are still  a handful of small mines like this  operating in scotland and wales  but their days too are numbered  the coal industry in england has been in  decline for years  the minor strikes and the pitched  battles over pit closures in the 1980s  couldn't save it and it's faced a slow  and painful death ever since  the last deep mine at kellingly in north  yorkshire closed five years ago  since then the only home produced coal  has come from open cast mines like  bradley  there is still coal here in concert but  owners banks group  says it's only commercially viable if  they can extend their operation  environmental campaigners fought hard  against those plans  and won the truth is we can't carry on  serving the purpose of profit at the  price of our lives  but sort of we have to make this  transition and we have to do all that we  can because our lives  in this planet is at stake economic  advances  matter for nothing on a dead planet  the company that mines this coal also  operates 10  onshore wind farms it argues that as  long as there is a need for it  the environmental cost of importing coal  is far greater  than that of produ